.ce
.B "EditPath in Kdenlive"
.sp .3
.ce
Kdenlive, Melt/MLT, dataset samples, and installation
.sp .3
.ce
Technical report — 24 July 2026
.sp 1
This report explains how EditPath instruments Kdenlive editing sessions, how
Kdenlive projects render through MLT and Melt, how raw commands become an
editor-process replay and completed dataset sample, and how to install the
stack on Linux, Windows, macOS, and containers.
.H1 "1. Executive summary"
EditPath is a capture, reconstruction, validation, and publication layer around
Kdenlive. Kdenlive remains the interactive editor. EditPath records semantic
commands, shortcuts, pointer gestures, project context, exact Kdenlive/MLT
state sidecars, checkpoint proxies, asset hashes, and immutable evidence.
.sp .3
Final media is rendered and validated independently. The editor replay is a
separate video made from the raw event stream plus reconstructed before/after
states; it is not a copy of the final movie.
.H1 "2. Capture-to-sample architecture"
.nf
Kdenlive + EditPath supervisor
  -> raw-events-NNN.jsonl + exact .kdenlive.zst states
  -> recovery/segment assembly and hash-chain validation
  -> verified content-addressed assets
  -> accepted-state reconstruction and Melt/MLT render
  -> render_edit_process(): command/state editor replay
  -> COMPLETED_SAMPLE/
.fi
.H1 "3. What is recorded"
.IP \(bu 2
Session envelope: IDs, schema, timestamps, completion, and recovery boundaries.
.IP \(bu 2
Project context: dimensions, FPS, aspect ratio, colorspace, audio, Kdenlive,
and MLT versions.
.IP \(bu 2
Raw UI evidence: ui.command, ui.shortcut, ui.gesture, transport actions, and
labels/targets in sequence order.
.IP \(bu 2
Semantic edits: state.diff before/after hashes and changed clips, tracks,
compositions, mixes, effects, and timeline duration.
.IP \(bu 2
Exact state: content-addressed compressed Kdenlive XML and checkpoint proxies.
.IP \(bu 2
Assets: stable IDs bound to copied files and SHA-256 hashes.
.H1 "4. MLT and Melt rendering"
MLT is the multimedia framework used by Kdenlive for producers, playlists,
tractors, transitions, filters, and consumers. Melt is MLT's command-line
multitrack/test editor. EditPath materializes the accepted Kdenlive state and
asks Melt to render it with the recorded project profile.
.IP 1 3
Load the accepted project state and bind each clip to a verified asset.
.IP 2 3
Build an MLT profile from width, height, FPS, progressive mode, colorspace,
and aspect-ratio fields in project.context.
.IP 3 3
Render through the avformat consumer, which uses FFmpeg libraries for encoding.
.IP 4 3
Probe with FFprobe and compare duration, dimensions, streams/audio, and visual
similarity against the editor's reference render.
.IP 5 3
Quarantine failures caused by missing services, assets, state hashes, or media
quality gates.
.CODE
melt reconstructed.kdenlive \\
  -consumer avformat:reconstructed.mp4 \\
  vcodec=libx264 acodec=aac real_time=-1
.END_CODE
The exact command depends on the encoder and project. Kdenlive projects may
also require avformat, xml, qimage/Qt, title, frei0r, Lottie, and other MLT
modules.
.H1 "5. How the editor replay is made"
The implementation is in `edit_path/process_video.py`.
.IP 1 3
`build_replay_steps()` selects the accepted branch baseline and state changes,
then associates each step with a reconstructed semantic snapshot.
.IP 2 3
`build_replay_moments()` walks raw events in sequence order and joins gestures
and shortcuts to nearby state diffs, creating before/after snapshots.
.IP 3 3
`_render_scene()` draws the editor-training view: project bin, project monitor,
effects/properties, multitrack timeline, transport, pointer/keyboard feedback,
command cards, selected clips, and semantic apply cards.
.IP 4 3
When exact source media exists, `render_event()` uses Melt/MLT for a state
preview. If media or an MLT service is missing, the renderer records a warning
and retains the semantic editor view.
.IP 5 3
Scenes are encoded frame-by-frame, concatenated, probed, and checked against
the expected quantized duration. The replay report records moments, states,
accepted edits, event counts, preview quality, warnings, and output hash.
.H1 "6. Completed sample layout"
.CODE
COMPLETED_SAMPLE/
  sample.json                  authoritative entry point
  output/final.mp4             editor reference video
  edit-path/events.jsonl       canonical raw trajectory
  edit-path/replay.mp4         editor-process replay
  internal/final.kdenlive      portable reconstructed project
  provenance/                  original project/reference evidence
  assets/                      verified content-addressed media
  metadata/                    session, asset, and replay reports
  evidence/                    immutable raw segments
  bundle-manifest.json         SHA-256/byte manifest
.END_CODE
.H1 "7. Installation"
.H2 "Linux: AppImage or Flatpak"
Download Kdenlive from the official download page. For an AppImage, make it
executable and launch it:
.CODE
chmod +x Kdenlive-*.AppImage
./Kdenlive-*.AppImage
.END_CODE
The GUI bundle does not automatically guarantee a discoverable EditPath
reconstruction runtime. Install/verify Melt/MLT, FFmpeg, FFprobe, Python, and
Zstandard; install the complete MLT module set, including avformat, xml, and
qimage/Qt services.
.CODE
python3 -m edit_path doctor
melt --version
ffmpeg -version
ffprobe -version
.END_CODE
.H2 "Windows 10/11 x64"
The repository documents a portable `EditPath-Windows-x64.zip` artifact. Extract
it and launch `EditPath.exe`, not adjacent Kdenlive directly; the bundle carries
instrumented Kdenlive, Melt/MLT, FFmpeg/FFprobe, Python, and reconstruction code.
The engineering artifact is unsigned, so SmartScreen may warn.
.sp .2
For a source setup, use PowerShell/Python 3, keep Melt and FFmpeg on PATH, and
run `py -3 -m edit_path doctor`. MLT's Windows build uses Git, MinGW, and CMake.
.H2 "macOS"
Kdenlive supplies Intel and Apple Silicon downloads for supported macOS. The
EditPath repository currently treats macOS as a native source-build path rather
than a signed app bundle. Install Qt/KDE/MLT/FFmpeg dependencies, build EditPath,
then run:
.CODE
EDIT_PATH_REPO_ROOT="$PWD" ./build/bin/EditPath
python3 -m edit_path doctor
.END_CODE
Docker Desktop can host reconstruction when a native runtime is inconvenient.
.H2 "Containers, CI, and WSL"
The pinned `reconstruction/Containerfile` installs Kdenlive/MLT modules,
FFmpeg/AVFilter, audio backends, Python, and fonts.
.CODE
docker build -t edit-path-reconstruction -f reconstruction/Containerfile .
docker run --rm --init -v "$PWD/session:/session:ro" \\
  -v "$PWD/dataset:/dataset" edit-path-reconstruction \\
  edit-path process /session /dataset
.END_CODE
Use `--init` to reap child processes. On openSUSE/WSL, QtMultimedia QML
imports are mandatory for Kdenlive's timeline; frei0r plugins and DejaVu or
Liberation fonts are also called out for headless scenes.
.H1 "8. Diagnostics and reproducibility"
`edit_path doctor` must find Melt (or mlt-melt), FFmpeg, FFprobe, Zstandard,
and a usable encoder. A runtime lock can pin Melt/MLT, FFmpeg, encoder, and
container identity. Keep the runtime consistent across capture, reconstruction,
and replay when visual comparison matters.
.IP \(bu 2
Hash mismatch: inspect recovery boundaries and the exact state chain.
.IP \(bu 2
Missing MLT service: install complete modules, not only the Melt executable.
.IP \(bu 2
Missing media: retain immutable assets and manifest hashes; otherwise exact
previews may degrade while semantic replay remains available.
.IP \(bu 2
Qt/headless failure: configure the Qt platform/plugin path and install fonts.
.H1 "9. Operator checklist"
.IP 1 3
Launch the EditPath supervisor and edit in Kdenlive.
.IP 2 3
Close Kdenlive after the independent final render completes.
.IP 3 3
Run `edit_path inspect` and `edit_path doctor`.
.IP 4 3
Run `edit_path process SESSION DATASET` with the intended Melt/MLT runtime.
.IP 5 3
Review render and replay reports, then validate `sample.json` and the manifest.
.IP 6 3
Human-QA `edit-path/replay.mp4`: commands, shortcuts, gestures, semantic edits,
and timeline states should be visible.
.H1 "10. References"
.IP \(bu 2
EditPath: https://github.com/Parsewave-internal/edit-path
.IP \(bu 2
Kdenlive downloads: https://kdenlive.org/download/
.IP \(bu 2
Kdenlive installation: https://docs.kdenlive.org/en/getting_started/installation.html
.IP \(bu 2
Melt documentation: https://www.mltframework.org/docs/melt/
.IP \(bu 2
MLT installation: https://www.mltframework.org/docs/install/
.sp .5
Check the linked official pages before production deployment because release
versions and platform requirements change.
